.TH GXWI-SD-EDITOR 1 "2026-10-05" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-sd-editor \- edit a security descriptor in a dialog on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-sd-editor
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opened by another program, which wants the security descriptor of something
of its own edited: a file, a registry key, a service. That program starts it
as a child, on the desktop it is on, and the two speak over the editor's
standard input and output, one JSON object a line. The program's first line
says what the object is called, what kind of thing it is, what its rights are
called, which parts of its descriptor it read, and what its descriptor is.
The editor shows it in a dialog of its own. When the person presses Apply or
OK, the editor writes the whole descriptor back, with the parts of it that
changed, and the program applies it and answers whether it could. The editor
changes nothing itself.
.PP
The whole descriptor is shown and edited. Access & Auditing lists everyone
the descriptor names; for whoever is picked, Access has a box for each general
right the program named, allowed and denied, where the rule applies on a
container, and the rights one at a time; Conditionals holds the rules that
apply only while a condition on claims or group membership holds; Specific
Rights holds rules for particular parts of the object, where the program
names its parts, with the rights a part takes, or says how a part is named
(an eventd field by its name, say); and Auditing holds what is recorded. Claims are the object's
resource claims, and Central Access Policy the policies it names. Effective
Access works out what someone could do with it, and why, as KACS would. The
owner, primary group, integrity label and process trust label are in the
dropdown under the owner's name.
.PP
Advanced Mode shows the access list and the SACL entry by entry, as KACS reads
them, and changes, moves (with the buttons, Alt+Up and Alt+Down, or by dragging),
duplicates and removes entries; and the descriptor's owner, group, control flags
and SDDL. An entry the other tabs cannot show is kept where it was found, and
shown here. An entry nobody changed goes back to the program exactly as it came.
.PP
The whole descriptor can be edited as SDDL text, which libpeios reads. An entry
left as it was in the text is the one it was, and goes back as it came. An entry
of a type SDDL has no code for is a comment such as
.B /* #1 kept as found: type 0x16, 12 bytes */
: left in, the entry stays where the comment is; taken out, the entry goes. The
text can change only the parts the program can.
.PP
What the program cannot change is shown and not offered, and the dialog says
why; where it can change nothing, Close is the only button. The owner can be
given only to whom KACS allows: the person themselves, a group they may own
things as, or anyone with SeRestorePrivilege. Who the person is comes from the
editor's own token, which is the program's.
.PP
On a container whose program says it can, changing what the container
passes down asks, on Apply or OK, whether to update what is already inside
it too. Things made inside afterwards get the change by themselves; things
already there keep what they were given unless they are updated. Updating
is the program's to do, as PCDS \(sc5.6 sets out: the dialog shows how far it
has got and can stop it, and afterwards says what could not be updated and
why.
.PP
Where a claim or a value is typed, the editor suggests the claims the machine
defines first, then those rules applied here have used, most used first. Both
are kept in the registry, as described under CLAIM CATALOGUE.
.PP
The editor ends when the dialog is closed or cancelled, and after OK once the
program has applied what it sent. It ends as well when its standard input
closes, and when the program that started it does.
.PP
Started by hand, with no request on its standard input, it says so and ends.
.SH CLAIM CATALOGUE
The machine's claims are kept at
.B Machine\eCommon\eSecurityDescriptorBuilder
for any program that builds security descriptors. Common holds formats that
are stable but not part of the Peios specifications. The key comes with the
editor's package, and the descriptor it takes from the machine hive lets
Administrators and SYSTEM change it and anyone signed in read it. The editor
never makes it: where it is missing, there are no suggestions.
.TP
.B KnownClaims\e\fISource\fB.\fIName\fR
One key a claim the machine defines, named as conditions name it:
.I Source
is User, Device, Resource or Local, as in User.Department or
Resource.Classification. Administrators add them. Each key may hold:
.RS
.TP
.B Type
(REG_SZ) The claim's kind as SDDL writes it: TS text, TI number, TU number
0 or more, TB yes or no, TD users or groups, TX bytes. A resource claim named
in the Claims tab is made this kind.
.TP
.B Values
(REG_MULTI_SZ) The values it takes, most likely first, suggested in that order.
.TP
.B Description
(REG_SZ) What it is for, shown beside its name.
.RE
.TP
.B UsedClaims
(REG_MULTI_SZ, a value of the key itself) The claims applied rules have used,
one a string: the claim as
.IR Source . Name ,
how many times it has been used, then each value it was used with as
.IR value = count ,
separated by tabs. A program counts a claim when a rule testing it is applied,
so a typo is never counted. Only a program that may write the key counts, so
the counts are of what Administrators have applied.
.SH SEE ALSO
The protocol is set out in the Peios userspace protocols (PSPU), on
learn.peios.org.
